WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.070 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hey  will you stop shouting you're scaring  the bears  i'm sorry what the bears  you're scaring them stop making those  those noises  this exchange happened at a zoo in  anchorage alaska  in august of 2014.  now to be clear this woman's  unsolicited feedback wasn't an  aberration  from the norm for me i was diagnosed  in sixth grade so i've had to deal with  interactions like these for  most of my life the timing  of this particular exchange though it  stung  and it stung because it happened at the  tail end of one of the most  transformative summers  of my life i had spent the  70 days leading up to that afternoon  biking more than 4 000 miles on a  bicycle not a motorcycle  from austin texas to anchorage alaska  the world's longest charity bike ride  around every switchback in the ozarks  through every cornfield in the midwest  and  up every peak in the canadian rockies  i listened to stories  stories shared with me by my teammates  as we rode  stories shared by host families in their  living rooms  and stories shared by complete strangers  in gas station parking lots  their stories inspired me to reflect  on my own and it was through that  deep hard reflection that i learned  how to love my story now  i'm still figuring out how to tell my  story  but i made a lot of progress that summer  progress that the woman from the zoo  couldn't have been more disinterested in  no she was worried about the bears  tourette's is a neurological condition  characterized by  involuntary vocal outbursts or motor  spasms called  ticks and it typically stems from  dysfunctional  basal ganglia a group of subcortical  nuclei  buried deep in the cerebral hemispheres  of the brain  responsible in part for motor and vocal  decision  making now for those of you who don't  have tourette's  and statistically speaking that's the  majority of you  let me break that down  think about the last time you were  working in a quiet coffee shop  chances are you didn't break the silence  in that coffee shop  by shouting unintentionally  think about the last time you were  taking handwritten notes  either in class or on a call  chances are you didn't put pen to paper  with such unintentional force  that the pens inc bled through the page  think about the last time you were hand  in hand  with someone else be it a friend  a family member or a romantic partner  chances are you didn't send  guilt-inducing  pain through that person's hand  by unintentionally squeezing their  fingers  in between yours and the reason you  didn't do  any of these things is because the  nuclei  in your basal ganglia are sending  inhibitory  signals through the thalamus and into  the cerebral cortex  to suppress those unwanted actions  you can picture the nuclei saying don't  shout at the coffee shop  don't slam the pen down  don't squeeze their fingers  i on the other hand have done these  things  in fact i still do a lot of these things  today you see  my brain isn't quite as reliable  at suppressing those unwanted actions  tourette's is tricky in that there's  still  a lot that the medical community doesn't  know  about the condition primarily because it  shows up  in disparate and nuanced ways  ticks certainly aren't consistent across  folks with tourette's  and seldom are they consistent for a  single person  with tourette's i woke up  one morning in middle school making  hiccup-like noises i couldn't control  these hiccups grew in both intensity  and frequency and were eventually  accompanied  by throat clearing  and nose blowing  i've had more than two dozen types of  ticks  since i was diagnosed some have come and  gone  others have remained inconveniently  loyal  middle school was pretty forlorn for me  as if the psychological and biological  turmoil that characterizes puberty  wasn't enough as is  i had been thrown the world's nastiest  curveball  and rather than squaring my shoulders to  home plate  and swinging at that curveball  i stepped away from the batter's box  because that was the easy thing to do  i relinquished my seat at the front of  the classroom  i walked along the edges of the hallways  in complete silence  and i declined invitations to birthday  parties  which were admittedly far and few  between for a middle schooler with  tourette's  because i didn't want the few friends i  did have  knowing just how different i was  i just wanted to feel like i belonged  we all do right  three years after my diagnosis my mom  sat me down the summer before i started  high school  and said kevin i know middle school was  tough  but high school doesn't have to be that  way  you can keep living under this rock and  i know that's the safe  and comfortable thing to do and no one  would blame you for it  or you can climb to the top of that rock  and start owning the things that make  you  you my mom was right  i have tourette's but tourette's doesn't  have  me tourette's is one of many parts of me  and it's a part of me i've grown to  appreciate  over the years you know  there's a lot that makes you you  as well neurodiversity  refers to the many intricate and complex  ways  in which the human brain differs from  one person to the next  sometimes those differences are subtle  and  other times they are frustratingly  pronounced  but they're always there and they  always make us stronger  and it's for that reason that each of us  has a vested interest in creating a  world  in which neurodiversity our differences  are both appreciated and celebrated  and there are three things that we can  do together  today and every day  to help cultivate that world  to help normalize what is truly  quite normal  first question  first impressions  by definition they're based off limited  sets of data as humans  we are wired to categorize people places  and things  safe or not safe same  or different likable  or not likeable now i'm not asking you  to refrain from having first impressions  or making snap judgments altogether  i am however asking you to question  their accuracy and in doing so  whether you know it or not you are  acknowledging  that there is always more to someone's  story  which leads me to point number two  learn someone's story learn everyone's  story and learn stories by having tough  conversations  by asking thoughtful questions  by doing research and sharing what you  learn  with others learning stories  can be hard it can be taxing and  exhausting  and time consuming but it is  always worth it so  roll up your sleeves and put in the time  and read other people's stories with  grace  and empathy with compassion  and curiosity you might just learn  something  and third actively expand  your in-group we're inherently drawn to  folks  who are similar to us we find  immense amounts of comfort in  homogeneity  but we learn and grow when we're  surrounded by folks  who aren't like us when we're surrounded  by folks  who look act think pray love  and yes even navigate zoos  differently than we do  so lean into that discomfort  embrace that uncertainty push those  boundaries everyone wants to feel  like they belong so let's make them  feel like they belong  if someone came up to me today  and said that with a wave of their magic  wand  they could send me back to sixth grade  and i could live my life  free of tourettes i wouldn't consider it  not even for a second sure  it'd be more convenient but having  tourette's has shaped who i am as a  person  it's colored the lens through which i  view the world  and it's informed how i treat the people  i'm lucky enough to navigate  this beautifully diverse world with  you might scare the bears every now and  then and that's okay  your story is uniquely yours  and my story is uniquely mine  so let's keep writing and sharing our  stories  with this world and let's do that  [Applause]  [Music]  together

